content.text:
The blocker is `min-width: auto` on flex items — for a replaced element it resolves to the intrinsic width (226px), and `min-width` always beats `max-width`. That's why nothing you try shrinks it.

Least CSS:

```css
img {
  height: auto;
  min-width: 0;
}
```

- `min-width: 0` releases the floor so `flex-shrink` can act.
- `height: auto` stops the squash — the `height="113"` attribute is a presentational hint setting a fixed height, so width shrinks while height doesn't.

The 226px cap comes free: `flex-basis: auto` resolves to the intrinsic width and `flex-grow` defaults to `0`, so it never exceeds it. No `max-width` needed.

That replaces both your `display: block` (redundant — flex items are blockified) and `min-width: 150px`. Want me to make the edit?

