Right, and scoping it surfaces a bug in what I wrote.

Neither `pezzos-hero-pieces.webc` nor `pezzos-hero.webc` uses `webc:root="override"` (`about-hero.webc` does), so both custom element tags stay in the output — that's exactly what makes your `:host` rule work. Which means the DOM is:

```
.graphics > pezzos-hero-pieces > .pieces
```

So `grid.parentElement` is **`<pezzos-hero-pieces>`**, not `.graphics`. And since `:host { display: block }` makes it wrap the grid tightly, it's the same box — you're not getting the larger hit area you asked for.

Fix both at once:

```js
var host = document.querySelector("pezzos-hero-pieces");
if (!host) return;

var grid = host.querySelector(".pieces");
if (!grid) return;

var field = host.parentElement || host;
```

Scopes the lookup, and `field` becomes `.graphics` without naming it. Apply?

